{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines","id":1541820,"title":"[V20] Mechanics for teaching Disciplines?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-09-09T17:07:37-07:00","id":1541820,"title":"[V20] Mechanics for teaching Disciplines?","content":"Running a V20 campaign, and I wanted to know if there are any mechanical rules for teaching Disciplines? Not the whole, feeding a blood point to the student, and more a barrier before a Kindred can spread Discipline knowledge. I am currently using a house rule stating that a Kindred must have four dots in a Discipline in order to teach that skill to another Kindred, based off the idea of specialties, but I'm unsure if there is already a mechanical rule behind it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28584-matter911","name":"Matter911","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-09T17:41:17-07:00","id":1541821,"title":"","content":"First, we need to make a distinction between &quot;natural&quot; and &quot;constructed&quot; disciplines. Long story short, for the purposes of what I have to say that's &quot;not blood sorcery&quot; and &quot;blood sorcery&quot;. I'm just using shorthand here on out.<br /> <br /> There aren't any rules like you describe except for &quot;constructed&quot; disciplines. Those are special, because they're not innate to the vampiric condition. But even then, it's not so much the ability to use blood sorceries that has to be taught, so much as the metaphysics and methodology of each blood sorcery form -- the difference between Thaumaturgy, Akhu, Koldunism, Dur-an-Ki, Sadhana, and the like.<br /> <br /> For &quot;natural&quot; disciplines -- even proprietary disciplines -- it's generally understood to be an intuitive, or instinctual, process. The capability to learn and use disciplines is innate to the blood; any vampire can learn and use any discipline. But, most of that capability is latent, and beyond a vampire's clan disciplines it must be activated (by drinking the blood of another vampire with the desired discipline). Once that capability is active, it develops naturally over time<br /> <br /> Remember, experience points and spending them to raise disciplines, are simply a game mechanic to represent a vampire's evolution.<br /> <br /> Short scenes, vignettes, and blue booking to narrate a vampire developing awakened talents is always great, but there's no published requirement to do so. It is, however, greatly incentivized by knocking an experience point or two off the experience cost for genuine, inventive, or simply amusing narration.<br /> <br /> For example, when the last Setite I played bought Serpentis 2, I spent a good chunk of a scene narrating how my character was walking around in the dark like an idiot, bumping into things and licking random objects to see if they tasted any different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 09-09-2024, 08:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-09T18:23:40-07:00","id":1541824,"title":"","content":"Yeah, despite the name, Disciplines are less structured knowledge and more understanding the properties of the Blood.<br /> <br /> Think about them as a middle ground between Talents and Skills, but by no means Knowledge. How much it fits towards one or the other, varies on a case by case basis.<br /> <br /> I don't think that requiring 4 dots to be a teacher makes a lot of sense for this, but you do you as they say. It theoretically has more to do with the power being manifest in the blood taken than the &quot;teacher&quot; really imparting any information of value.<br /> <br /> Assuming you really want to stick to this, maybe it would be better to demand an Occult roll from the teacher instead. \u0130t would represent their ability to translate the stirrings of the Blood into a proper language to convey what kind of effort the student must do.<br /> <br /> Better yet, it could be a completely new Ability, either a Skill or Knowledge called Discipline Teaching, and to teach you roll Discipline + Teaching. That would make teachers rarer and valuable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T00:38:05-07:00","id":1541838,"title":"","content":"Agreed with the others about &quot;normal&quot; (read: non-Blood Sorcery) Disciplines being more intuitive. A teacher might give pointers, which might translate to learning a new dot faster (ST's discretion), but ultimately it's not a trick that's taught. It's a capability inherent to the vampire's physiology.<br /> <br /> To lift a phrase from Hellsing, &quot;blood is the currency of the soul&quot;. It carries the legacy of the Kindred's progenitors inside it. They may not remember how to use a Discipline power. But the Blood does.<br /> <br /> Learning a Discipline power is the acting of coaxing the Blood into revealing its secrets.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T04:46:30-07:00","id":1541848,"title":"","content":"Thank you guys for your responses. I think I will lift the whole, four dot requirement, as I was mainly worried about my players all sharing their Disciplines with one another, but then it doesn't make sense given what teaching Disciplines is, storywise.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines?p=1541821#post1541821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Short scenes, vignettes, and blue booking to narrate a vampire developing awakened talents is always great, but there's no published requirement to do so. It is, however, greatly incentivized by knocking an experience point or two off the experience cost for genuine, inventive, or simply amusing narration.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, I really like this idea, I don't know if this is in the rulebook and I missed it or a common house rule, but I'm going to add this to my game moving forward.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28584-matter911","name":"Matter911","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T07:00:04-07:00","id":1541860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines?p=1541824#post1541824\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Better yet, it could be a completely new Ability, either a Skill or Knowledge called Discipline Teaching, and to teach you roll Discipline + Teaching. That would make teachers rarer and valuable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Already existed in older editions, it was dummied out because it got very overpowered and broke game balance very quickly. Used to be a secondary skill called instruction, if I remember right. Difficulties were all over the place and varied heavily on edition; the outcome was the student got a bonus experience point per success, usable only for the trait being taught (or reduced that experience cost by 1 per success).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matter911</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines?p=1541848#post1541848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thank you guys for your responses. I think I will lift the whole, four dot requirement, as I was mainly worried about my players all sharing their Disciplines with one another, but then it doesn't make sense given what teaching Disciplines is, storywise.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's honestly not that big of a deal. Remember, clans aren't classes and disciplines alone do not a character make. Out of clan disciplines become cost-prohibitive very fast, unless a player has something very specific in mind -- and in some cases, dipping out of clan is ultimately beneficial for the coterie. Having more than one character with Auspex, for example, can be a lifesaver.<br /> <br /> The only use case in which it becomes undeniably problematic, is when or if every PC learns mid- to high-level Celerity. Then you just run into the &quot;combat rounds take an hour to resolve&quot; problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T12:30:45-07:00","id":1541879,"title":"","content":"Another way to think about teaching disciplines is to equate them to martial art styles.<br /> <br /> A Karate practitioner moves and fights in a completely different way than say a Judo practitioner, despite both having the same physical body plan of two arms, two legs, and one head. Clan Disciplines are just the martial art style that your body type is naturally inclined towards, sort of like how big bodied individuals tend to favor tanking/blocking hits while lighter framed fighters favor dodging hits.<br /> <br /> Training someone in a new style just takes time so they don't suffer from style confusion, watching someone who was trained to do a side fall get confused and try to do a roll and accomplish neither is funny but painful to see happen. The same thing with disciplines, if they already know Potence they understand how to focus their strength, but learning Celerity requires them to learn how to relax and flow instead.<br /> <br /> I agree that magic disciplines like Thaumaturgy and Necromancy require more traditional book learning and formula and thus have to be taught in a different way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T13:42:47-07:00","id":1541882,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matter911</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines#post1541820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Running a V20 campaign, and I wanted to know if there are any mechanical rules for teaching Disciplines? Not the whole, feeding a blood point to the student, and more a barrier before a Kindred can spread Discipline knowledge.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know in V20 it states that one must drink the blood of someone who already knows that Discipline in order to begin learning a new out-of-clan Discipline, but I do not believe that was a requirement in previous editions. Lots of people did have it as a house rule, especially in LARP. The main reason for that seemed to be that the distinctions between clans seemed to quickly fade in LARP as certain Disciplines were far more useful in a LARP setting than others. For simple character survival, Potence 5 was essential while powers like Dominate 4 or 5 did not have much use in LARP. So most LARP PCs regardless of clan developed Potence. So it quickly looked like every vampire in a city was a Brujah. Nobody was very happy with that, so STs introduced obstacles to it (which only caused it to happen slower, not eliminate it). LARPs usually handed out XP at a higher rate than tabletop chronicles, so that was another reason.<br /> <br /> I saw less use of that in tabletop, but even here certain Disciplines were favored by certain gaming groups (Potence, again, was always popular especially with new players who were used to more combat heavy RPGs) so some STs created house rules in an attempt to clamp down on it so vampires of different clans seemed like they were different.<br /> <br /> I always thought that was the wrong solution, but it was a popular one.<br /> <br /> Another issue was the signature Disciplines of certain clans. Gangrel &quot;owned&quot; Protean; Tremere &quot;owned&quot; Thaumaturgy; Lasombra &quot;owned&quot; Obtenebration; and so on. Since the Sabbat Clans and Independent Clans were meant to be mysterious and unknown, many STs and players didn't want such powers to be known by others. That would &quot;ruin&quot; the game and make those clans and sects less special. Of course, the setting developed in ways that they became less special anyway, and it seemed (to me at least) that attempts to prevent other vampires from learning them just gimped those clans which did not have signature Disciplines. But this was often seen as an issue, so STs introduced house rules so that PCs could not learn these proprietary Disciplines unless you had special teachers, drank their blood, etc.<br /> <br /> Another common house rule was that certain Disciplines - almost always the physical ones of Celerity, Foritude, and Potence - could be learned more easily since they were &quot;natural&quot;, but the other Disciplines required teachers or something special. Some players even thought Potence could be learned by simply working out as if vampires had living cells and Potence came from muscles. That always seemed bizarre to me, but some variant of it shows up a lot.<br /> <br /> Instead I always thought Disciplines (outside of the magical ones that had Paths and Rituals) were all equally innate to the vampire, and could be developed naturally. Yeah, having mentors, teachers, and coaches can help, but that was more flavor text than anything else. In all my favorite tabletop gaming groups, our rule was if you had the XP you could buy the Discipline. You just had to put some effort into the flavor text to explain why. If the group and ST liked it, we went with it. If the reason seemed weak, we'd tell the player to do better (and usually helped her with ideas). Originally we still made Sabbat and Independent clan Disciplines the exception, but eventually we realized that it made little sense and simply accepted that such Disciplines should be made available to the original seven clans of the Camarilla and Anarchs as well. The game setting by that point had so many exceptions that pretending those powers should not be out there lost its rationale.<br /> <br /> When it came to powers that had Paths and Rituals, I always kept those under special control as ST. Because magic was not an innate power of the blood, but was based on special knowledge that simply used the inherent magical properties of vitae. something very different. However, not all Paths could be learned by anyone. Certain Paths were known among the vampire community in general (I usually said that powers the genre usually gave vampires like Movement of Mind, Lure of Flames, and Weather Control was because the secrets were out there provided you can find a tutor or resources. Other Paths were known exclusively to large groups (common Tremere Paths among Tremere like Path of Blood). Other Paths were exclusive to small groups (these particular packs of Sabbat; this particular chantry of Tremere; these followers and allies of this specific vampire).<br /> <br /> There is already a &quot;penalty&quot; for learning out-of-clan Disciplines - higher XP cost. I don't think there should be anything else. Requirements like forcing a vampire to drink the blood of another to activate learning just causes players to strategically choose clans for their PCs or mentors that reduce the cost overall to them. It becomes artificial and forced. It doesn't actually create a better game or make things more appropriate for the genre.<br /> <br /> If a Malkavian decides he wants to learn Potence and Protean, that's a valid choice. As is a Brujah learning Dominate and Auspex. <br /> <br /> Yeah, we'd all like to see &quot;recognizable&quot; clans, but in tabletop most characters will be NPCs anyway so the ST can allow PCs to be a bit special and still preserve the clan. And you can emphasize clan culture a lot so that characters are more distinctively members of their clan. STs have less control of this in LARP where NPCs are few and PCs make up most of the game so I at least understand why they tend to be heavy handed.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T13:56:13-07:00","id":1541883,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines?p=1541860#post1541860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Used to be a secondary skill called instruction, if I remember right. Difficulties were all over the place and varied heavily on edition; the outcome was the student got a bonus experience point per success, usable only for the trait being taught (or reduced that experience cost by 1 per success).</div> </div> </div> </div>Instruction was a secondary talent in 1e and 2e Players Guides, but as written only allows someone to teach their Skills and Knowledges to another up to their ranks. So it didn't apply to Talents (though STs could allow certain Talents) and it certainly did not apply to Disciplines. <br /> <br /> As written it did seem to allow PCs to learn those Abilities without spending XP, but I believe the rules were really meant to be used at teaching NPCs (who obviously did not earn XP), not PCs (who did receive XP and thus could improve by whatever means they develop in game, which could be by learning from a teacher who had the Instruction ability). However, I would not be surprised if many gaming groups simply used it as a way to increase abilities without XP which is broken.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T14:17:02-07:00","id":1541885,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines?p=1541860#post1541860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Already existed in older editions, it was dummied out because it got very overpowered and broke game balance very quickly.</div> </div> </div> </div> I know, and the whole XP benefit was the crux of the problem, although in general it ended up being poorly thought and ultimately scrapped.<br /> <br /> But for a game where the importance of an adequate teacher is played up, having such an Ability isn't a bad idea. It doesn't actually need rules, even, just to be a requirement for teaching.<br /> <br /> That's for a very specific effect on play, though, I don't think it is at all necessary for most chronicles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541820-v20-mechanics-for-teaching-disciplines?p=1541882#post1541882\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I know in V20 it states that one must drink the blood of someone who already knows that Discipline in order to begin learning a new out-of-clan Discipline, but I do not believe that was a requirement in previous editions.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure I've seen it as an optional rule at least as early as Revised Core book, though it may have been in a Second Edition book before that.<br /> <br /> But it's like most effects of Diablerie, an option never really presented as an actual rule, that most books simply don't use, yet it got such a traction among players that loads of people think to be RAW.<br /> <br /> All in all, in the TTRPG this is an optional solution to a non problem. It's like the Teaching Ability: it can serve well if you want to play a specific kind of game, for how it affects the narrative, but otherwise just scrap it. For LARP idk.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T14:53:09-07:00","id":1541888,"title":"","content":"Something Mage did that made Instruction valuable without as many problems:<br /> <br /> It coupled it with optional learning times for Spheres. Appropriate Backgrounds or characters with the Instruction ability could reduce learning times rather than muck with XP.<br /> <br /> While not many people like learning times in the WoD (XP costs going up as high as they can frequently makes them feel superfluous or punishing depending on implementation), it can help alleviate concerns if a group wants some more systems in this regard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}